{=============================================================================
  Ideal Stirling cycle with a perfect regenerator: work, heat and efficiency
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0059
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Theoretical Stirling cycle (two isotherms and two isochores) driven by an
  ideal regenerator. The minimum pressure and temperature of the cycle are
  100 kPa and 25 C, the volumetric compression ratio is 10 and the maximum
  temperature of the cycle is 1000 C. The four transformations of the cycle are
  analysed in terms of work and heat exchanged, the overall efficiency of the
  machine is computed and compared with the Carnot efficiency between the
  minimum and maximum temperatures of the cycle; the efficiency of the Stirling
  cycle without regenerator is computed as well.

  Every transformation is treated twice: with the perfect-gas model (indices
  without suffix) and with the real-gas air property functions (indices
  suffixed _bis, "as in the original"). The works of the two isotherms are also
  computed with the EES definite integral of p dv over the specific volume and
  compared with the closed form of the isothermal work.

  Inputs : r_v (volumetric compression ratio), p[1] (minimum pressure),
           T[1] (minimum temperature), T[3] (maximum temperature), fluid$
  Outputs: p[i], T[i], v[i], s[i] (states), w_ij, q_ij (work and heat of each
           transformation), w_net, eta_stirling (no regenerator),
           eta_stirling_regen (with regenerator), eta_carnot and the _bis
           equivalents computed with the real-gas air properties
  Units  : SI - temperatures in C, pressures in Pa, energies in J
           (the original works in K, kPa and kJ/kg: converted by hand, see
            README.md)
  Source : ULiege, course "Thermodynamique appliquee" (MECA0002), repetition
           R05, exercise 3 (EES file R05_E03_2022.EES) - see README.md
  Variant: stirling_cycle_ideal_regenerator_coolsolve.eescode - this file is
           the runnable variant; same model, same physics, same results as
           stirling_cycle_ideal_regenerator.eescode, whose two definite
           integrals are rewritten (change of variable) because CoolSolve allows
           only one integration variable per model (CS-GAP-INTEGRAL-MULTIVAR) and
           only increasing limits (CS-GAP-INTEGRAL-DECREASING: w_12_bis integrates
           from v[1] to v[2] < v[1]). Every change is logged in README.md.
  This file is the runnable variant of the native file
  stirling_cycle_ideal_regenerator.eescode (read its header first); the native
  file cannot run on CoolSolve. The limitations it meets and the
  changes made here, and only here:
    1. the two definite integrals of the native file use the state volumes v12
       and v34 as integration variables, and CoolSolve allows a single
       integration variable per model (CS-GAP-INTEGRAL-MULTIVAR). Both integrals
       are therefore written on one integration variable x of the interval
       [0,1], the state volumes being mapped on it by the change of variable
       v = v_a + (v_b - v_a)*x: v12 = v[1] + (v[2]-v[1])*x and
       v34 = v[3] + (v[4]-v[3])*x; the integral runs over x instead of over v
       and the factor of the change of variable ((v[2]-v[1] resp. (v[4]-v[3]))
       is applied in its own equation;
    2. the factor (sign included) is applied in its own equation:
       w_12_bis = -w_int_12*(v[2]-v[1]);
    3. the integration step is 0.001 in x instead of 0.1 m^3/kg in v (0.1 m^3/kg
       is about 0.13 in x here): CoolSolve integrates with RK4 and is far more
       accurate than the trapezoidal rule of EES, so the results differ from the
       stored EES values by the quadrature error of EES (see README.md).
  No other equation, variable name, constant or input value is modified, and no
  CoolSolve-only syntax is used: this file is valid EES.
=============================================================================}

"!Data"
fluid$ = 'Air'    "working fluid [as in the original]"
r = 287           "specific gas constant of air [J/(kg-K)] (0.287 kJ/(kg-K) in the original); no equation of the original uses it"
r_bis = R#/MOLARMASS(Air_ha)   "R# is the universal gas constant [J/kmol-K]"
r_v = 10          "compression ratio / volumetric ratio [-]"
p[1] = 100E3      "minimum pressure of the cycle [Pa] (100 kPa in the original)"
p_bis[1] = p[1]
T[1] = 25         "minimum temperature of the cycle [C] (25 K + 273.15 K in the original, i.e. 298.15 K)"
T_bis[1] = T[1]
T[3] = 1000       "maximum temperature of the cycle [C] (1273.15 K in the original)"
T_bis[3] = T[3]
{as in the original: the _bis variables are used for the same calculation but
 treating air as a real gas}

"!Solution"

"!Isothermal compression 1-2 -> T = constant -> heat is REMOVED from the fluid during the compression"
v[1] = r_bis*(T[1]+273.15)/p[1]   "specific volume at 1, perfect gas law [m^3/kg]"
v_bis[1] = volume(Air_ha,T=T[1],P=p[1])   "specific volume at 1, real gas [m^3/kg]"

T[2] = T[1]       "isothermal transformation"
T_bis[2] = T[2]

v[2] = v[1]/r_v   "specific volume at 2, from the volumetric ratio [m^3/kg]"
v_bis[2] = v[2]

p[2] = r_bis*(T[2]+273.15)/v[2]   "pressure at 2, perfect gas law [Pa]"
p_bis[2] = pressure(Air_ha,T=T[2],v=v[2])   "pressure at 2, real gas [Pa]"

w_12 = r_bis*(T[1]+273.15)*ln(r_v)   "work required by the compression in a closed system: -integral of p dv [J/kg]"
p_integrale12 = pressure(Air_ha,T=T[2],v=v12)   "integrand of the real-gas compression work [Pa]"

{change of variable: the native file integrates p_integrale12 dv from v[1] to v[2] with the state volume v12 as integration variable; CoolSolve needs the constant limits 0 and 1 of the single integration variable x (see the header)}
v12 = v[1] + (v[2]-v[1])*x   "state volume of the compression integral [m^3/kg], mapped on x in [0,1]"
w_int_12 = 0 + integral(p_integrale12, x, 0, 1, 0.001)   "integral of p_integrale12 over x in [0,1] [Pa]"
w_12_bis = -w_int_12*(v[2]-v[1])   "compression work, real gas: -integral of p_integrale12 dv from v[1] to v[2] [J/kg]"

w_12 + q_12 = cv12*(T[2]-T[1])   "first law for a closed system to obtain the heat exchanged: q+w = du, which comes down to q_12 = -w_12 since the transformation is isothermal [J/kg]"
cv12 = cv(fluid$,T=(T[1]+T[2])/2)   "cv is assumed constant over the whole transformation, which would not be judicious for large temperature variations [J/(kg-K)]"
{as in the original: the internal energies u can be used instead; the result is not exactly the same because air (Air_ha) is treated as a real gas below}
w_12_bis + q_12_bis = u[2]-u[1]   "internal energy route [J/kg]"
u[1] = intenergy(Air_ha,T=T[1],P=p[1])   "internal energy at 1, real gas [J/kg]"
u[2] = intenergy(Air_ha,T=T[2],P=p[2])   "internal energy at 2, real gas [J/kg]"

"!Isochoric heating 2-3: v = constant"
{note: this quantity of heat, equal to q_41 in absolute value, can be recovered with a regenerator}
v[3] = v[2]       "isochoric transformation"
v_bis[3] = v[3]
w_23 = 0          "no work exchanged, isochoric transformation: -integral of p dv = 0 [J/kg]"
w_23_bis = 0

w_23 + q_23 = cv23*(T[3]-T[2])   "heat exchanged from the first law for closed systems [J/kg]"
p[3] = r_bis*(T[3]+273.15)/v[3]   "p_3, perfect gas law [Pa]"
p_bis[3] = pressure(Air_ha,T=T[3],v=v[3])   "p_3, real gas [Pa]"

cv23 = cv(fluid$,T=(T[2]+T[3])/2)   "cv is assumed constant over the whole transformation, which would not be judicious for large temperature variations [J/(kg-K)]"
{as in the original: the internal energies u can be used instead; the result is not exactly the same because air is treated as a real gas below}
w_23_bis + q_23_bis = u[3]-u[2]   "internal energy route [J/kg]"
u[3] = intenergy(Air_ha,T=T[3],v=v[3])   "internal energy at 3, real gas [J/kg]"

"!Isothermal expansion 3-4: T = constant, heat is ADDED to the fluid (which would tend to cool down during the expansion) !!!"
T[4] = T[3]       "isothermal transformation"
T_bis[4] = T[4]

p[4] = r_bis*(T[4]+273.15)/v[4]   "the specific volume at 4 is known (see below) -> p_4, perfect gas law [Pa]"
p_bis[4] = pressure(Air_ha,T=T[4],v=v[1])   "real gas: v_1 because an isochoric cooling follows"

w_34 = r_bis*(T[3]+273.15)*ln(v[3]/v[4])   "work exchanged during the isothermal expansion: -integral of p dv [J/kg]"
p_integrale34 = pressure(Air_ha,T=T[3],v=v34)   "integrand of the real-gas expansion work [Pa]"

{change of variable, as for the compression integral above}
v34 = v[3] + (v[4]-v[3])*x   "state volume of the expansion integral [m^3/kg], mapped on x in [0,1]"
w_int_34 = 0 + integral(p_integrale34, x, 0, 1, 0.001)   "integral of p_integrale34 over x in [0,1] [Pa]"
w_34_bis = -w_int_34*(v[4]-v[3])   "expansion work, real gas: -integral of p_integrale34 dv from v[3] to v[4] [J/kg]"

w_34 + q_34 = cv34*(T[4]-T[3])   "heat received during the expansion, from the first law for closed systems [J/kg]"
cv34 = cv(fluid$,T=(T[3]+T[4])/2)   "cv is assumed constant over the whole transformation, which would not be judicious for large temperature variations [J/(kg-K)]"
{as in the original: the internal energies u can be used instead; the result is not exactly the same because air is treated as a real gas below}
w_34_bis + q_34_bis = u[4]-u[3]   "internal energy route [J/kg]"
u[4] = intenergy(Air_ha,T=T[4],P=p[4])   "internal energy at 4, real gas [J/kg]"

"!Isochoric cooling 4-1: v = constant"
v[4] = v[1]
v_bis[4] = v[4]
w_41 = 0          "no work exchanged, isochoric transformation [J/kg]"
w_41_bis = 0

w_41 + q_41 = cv41*(T[1]-T[4])   "heat exchanged from the first law for closed systems [J/kg]"
cv41 = cv(fluid$,T=(T[1]+T[4])/2)   "cv is assumed constant over the whole transformation, which would not be judicious for large temperature variations [J/(kg-K)]"
{as in the original: the internal energies u can be used instead; the result is not exactly the same because air is treated as a real gas below}
w_41_bis + q_41_bis = u[1]-u[4]   "internal energy route [J/kg]"

"!Thermal efficiency"

"!Cycle WITHOUT regenerator -> two quantities of heat must be supplied: q_23 during the isochoric heating AND q_34 during the ISOTHERMAL expansion"
q_couteux = q_23 + q_34   "heat supplied to the cycle without regenerator [J/kg]"
eta_stirling = w_net/q_couteux   "efficiency without regenerator [-]"
w_net = -(w_34+w_12)   "net work = useful effect [J/kg]"
"!Cycle WITH regenerator -> only q_34 during the isothermal expansion must be supplied. The heat q_23 received during the isochoric heating is recovered from the isochoric cooling q_41"
q_c = q_34   "heat supplied to the cycle with regenerator [J/kg]"
eta_stirling_regen = w_net/q_c   "efficiency with regenerator [-]"
eta_stirling_regen_bis = -(w_34_bis+w_12_bis)/q_34_bis   "efficiency with regenerator, real gas [-]"

"!Carnot efficiency: the cold source is at T_1 and the hot source at T_3"
eta_carnot = 1-(T[1]+273.15)/(T[3]+273.15)   "Carnot efficiency between the minimum and maximum temperatures [-]"

{as in the original: the theoretical Stirling cycle WITH regenerator has the same
 efficiency as the Carnot cycle, and the efficiency of the cycle WITHOUT
 regenerator is worth almost 55 % of the Carnot efficiency}

"!For information"
s[1] = entropy('Air',T=T[1],p=p[1])   "entropy at 1 [J/(kg-K)]"
s[2] = entropy('Air',T=T[2],p=p[2])   "entropy at 2 [J/(kg-K)]"
s[3] = entropy('Air',T=T[3],p=p[3])   "entropy at 3 [J/(kg-K)]"
s[4] = entropy('Air',T=T[4],p=p[4])   "entropy at 4 [J/(kg-K)]"
